{
  "version": 1,
  "kind": "Graph",
  "name": "Undertow",
  "description": "Algorithmic: turbulence crossing a moving threshold drops the pulse rate onto random rungs; it climbs back at the speed of your motion",
  "author": "ShockingVRC",
  "graph": {
    "version": 1,
    "nodes": [
      {
        "id": 1,
        "pos": [
          40.0,
          -280.0
        ],
        "title": "Algorithmic · undertow",
        "kind": {
          "type": "Frame",
          "size": [
            720.0,
            250.0
          ],
          "text": "No clock. Drops come out of turbulence: a noise that churns faster with velocity, acceleration, recoil and depth.\nWhen: a drop starts when the turbulence — plus recoil spikes — rises past a threshold that sinks as you go deeper, and lasts until it calms below a second one, so long falls and short stabs come and go unpredictably.\nHow far: each drop lands on one of four rungs picked by its own noise, which velocity and acceleration stir; depth at that moment scales the rung, and the landing holds until the drop ends.\nHow it moves: the frequency snaps down in one tick, then climbs back at 40–320 Hz/s — acceleration and recoil set the climb. Intensity sinks over 200 ms as it falls and returns as it climbs; velocity deepens the dip, depth softens it, and depth and acceleration lift the top. Over minutes velocity tires the top frequency (95 → about 73 Hz); rest restores it.\nSliders: Max frequency drop caps every fall (Hz). Max device power is Limit Max on both channels — 6 to start; the Graph cap in Setup still wins. B follows 220 ms later at 85 % intensity."
        }
      },
